# NorthNarrow — escalation allowlist (BUG-032).
#
# process×destination tuples whose connections are EXCLUDED FROM THE
# COUNT of the COMBAT-tier exfiltration_pattern / lateral_movement
# heuristics (count-filter, NOT whole-trigger suppress): an allowlisted
# mirror / orchestration flow cannot inflate the threshold, but a real
# C2 connection — even from a process with a spoofed comm — still counts
# because its destination is not in the allowlisted CIDR.
#
# Format (one rule per line; blank lines + `#` comments ignored):
#
#   <trigger>  <comm|comm*>  <dst-cidr>  [port|*]
#
#   trigger : exfil | lateral
#   comm    : exact process name (<= 15 chars — the kernel truncates to
#             TASK_COMM_LEN) OR a trailing-'*' prefix glob. USE THE
#             PREFIX FORM for any name longer than 15 chars, e.g.
#             `systemd-resolved` -> `systemd-resolv*`, or an exact entry
#             will silently never match the truncated comm.
#   dst-cidr: IPv4 (or IPv6) network — `addr` or `addr/prefix`.
#   port    : optional; a number or `*` (any). Default: any.
#
# Fail-secure: this file absent/unreadable = EMPTY allowlist (nothing
# excluded). Malformed lines are skipped with a WARN — a bad line does
# not disable the rest of the file.
#
# OWNED LIMIT: because the filter keys on the destination, traffic to an
# allowlisted destination that an attacker abuses as C2 (e.g. exfil to a
# GitHub repo when GitHub ranges are listed) rides under the filter.
# Keep allowlisted ranges as tight as the legitimate use requires.
#
# Examples (commented out — uncomment + adapt to your environment):
# exfil    apt          91.189.91.0/24     443     # ubuntu archive mirror
# exfil    *            140.82.112.0/20    443     # github (any comm)
# lateral  ansible*     10.0.0.0/8         22      # config-mgmt ssh fan-out
